1. Windows Server Virtual Machine

I created a Windows Server virtual machine using VMware Workstation Pro.

General setup:

Download the Windows Server ISO
Create a new virtual machine in VMware
Attach the Windows Server ISO
Allocate CPU, memory, and storage
Install Windows Server
Configure the local Administrator account
Boot into Windows Server

At this point, the server is a standalone Windows Server and is not yet a Domain Controller.

2. Install Active Directory Domain Services

Active Directory Domain Services was installed through Server Manager.

Server Manager
→ Add Roles and Features
→ Active Directory Domain Services

AD DS provides centralized management of identities and resources within a Windows domain.

Examples of Active Directory objects include:

Users
Computers
Servers
Groups
Organizational Units
3. Promote the Server to a Domain Controller

Installing AD DS does not automatically make the server a Domain Controller.

After installing AD DS, I selected:

Promote this server to a domain controller

A new Active Directory forest and domain were then created.

Domain Controller

A Domain Controller runs Active Directory Domain Services and handles services such as domain authentication and directory management.

Domain

A domain contains directory objects such as users, computers, groups, and Organizational Units.

Forest

A forest is the highest level of an Active Directory environment. A forest can contain one or more domains.

4. Organizational Units

I created three primary Organizational Units based on geographic regions:

USA
Europe
SEAsia

Each regional OU contains:

Users
Computers
Servers

Example:

USA
├── Users
├── Computers
└── Servers

Organizational Units provide a logical structure for Active Directory objects.

OUs can be used to:

Organize users and computers
Apply Group Policy
Delegate administrative responsibilities
Separate resources by location or function
5. Active Directory Groups

Groups allow administrators to manage multiple users together instead of configuring permissions for every account individually.

For example:

User
↓
IT Security Group
↓
Resource Permission

A user's access can then be managed through group membership.

6. Group Scope

Active Directory provides three main group scopes.

Global

Global groups are commonly used to organize users from the same domain based on their role.

Example:

IT
Domain Local

Domain Local groups are commonly used when assigning permissions to resources within a domain.

Example:

DL_ITAdmin
Universal

Universal groups can contain members from multiple domains within a forest.

They become more useful in larger multi-domain environments.

7. Group Type
Security Groups

Security groups can be used to assign permissions to resources.

Examples include:

Shared folders
Applications
Printers
Administrative resources
Distribution Groups

Distribution groups are primarily used for email distribution.

They are not used to assign security permissions.

8. Creating Groups

Groups are created through Active Directory Users and Computers.

Active Directory Users and Computers
→ Select OU
→ New
→ Group

When creating a group, I configure:

Group name
Group scope
Group type

Users can then be added as members of the appropriate groups.

9. Creating Users

User accounts are created through Active Directory Users and Computers.

Active Directory Users and Computers
→ Users OU
→ New
→ User

The account is configured with:

Name
Logon name
Password
Password settings
Appropriate group membership

The resulting account becomes an identity managed by Active Directory.

